          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A ball of mass $m_1$ moving with velocity $u$ undergoes an elastic head-on collision with a stationary ball of mass $m_2$. After collision, the first ball comes to rest. The ratio of masses $\\\\frac{m_1}{m_2}$ is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$1$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$2$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$0.5$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$\\\\sqrt{2}$\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'In elastic 1D collision, $v_1 = \\\\frac{m_1 - m_2}{m_1 + m_2} u$. Since $v_1 = 0$, $m_1 = m_2$, so the ratio is 1.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 15,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section A\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'MCQ\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'The dimensions of permittivity of free space $\\\\varepsilon_0$ in terms of fundamental dimensions $[M, L, T, A]$ is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$[M^{-1} L^{-3} T^4 A^2]$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$[M^{-1} L^3 T^{-4} A^{-2}]$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$[M L^3 T^{-2} A^{-1}]$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$[M L^{-3} T^2 A^2]$\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Coulomb force $F = \\\\frac{q^2}{4\\\\pi \\\\varepsilon_0 r^2} \\\\implies [\\\\varepsilon_0] = \\\\frac{[A^2 T^2]}{[M L T^{-2}][L^2]} = [M^{-1} L^{-3} T^4 A^2]$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 16,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section A\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'MCQ\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A car is negotiating an unbanked circular curve of radius $r = 50\\\\text{ m}$ on a horizontal road. If the coefficient of static friction is $\\\\mu_s = 0.2$ and $g = 10\\\\text{ m/s}^2$, the maximum safe speed is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$10\\\\text{ m/s}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$14\\\\text{ m/s}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$20\\\\text{ m/s}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$5\\\\text{ m/s}$\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'$v_{\\\\max} = \\\\sqrt{\\\\mu_s g r} = \\\\sqrt{0.2 \\\\times 10 \\\\times 50} = \\\\sqrt{100} = 10\\\\text{ m/s}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 17,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section A\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'MCQ\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A wire of resistance $R$ is stretched uniformly such that its radius decreases to $\\\\frac{r}{2}$. The new resistance of the wire becomes:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$16 R$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$4 R$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$8 R$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$2 R$\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Volume $V = A L = \\\\text{constant}$. $R = \\\\rho \\\\frac{L}{A} = \\\\rho \\\\frac{V}{A^2}$. Since $A \\\\propto r^2$, $R \\\\propto \\\\frac{1}{r^4}$. Decreasing radius by 2 increases resistance by $2^4 = 16$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 18,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section A\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'MCQ\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'An inductor of self-inductance $L = 2\\\\text{ H}$ carries a current decreasing at a constant rate $\\\\frac{dI}{dt} = -5\\\\text{ A/s}$. The induced electromotive force (emf) across the inductor is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$+10\\\\text{ V}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$-10\\\\text{ V}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$2.5\\\\text{ V}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$0.4\\\\text{ V}$\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'$\\\\mathcal{E} = -L \\\\frac{dI}{dt} = -(2) \\\\times (-5) = +10\\\\text{ V}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 19,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section A\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'MCQ\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A Carnot engine has an efficiency of $\\\\eta = 40\\\\%$ when its sink is at $T_c = 300\\\\text{ K}$. To increase the efficiency to $50\\\\%$, keeping sink temperature constant, the source temperature must be raised by:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$100\\\\text{ K}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$50\\\\text{ K}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$200\\\\text{ K}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$150\\\\text{ K}$\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Initial: $1 - \\\\frac{300}{T_h} = 0.4 \\\\implies T_h = 500\\\\text{ K}$. Desired: $1 - \\\\frac{300}{T_h\\\'} = 0.5 \\\\implies T_h\\\' = 600\\\\text{ K}$. Rise $\\\\Delta T_h = 600 - 500 = 100\\\\text{ K}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 20,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section A\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'MCQ\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A plane electromagnetic wave propagating along the $+z$ direction has its electric field given by $\\\\vec{E} = E_0 \\\\cos(kz - \\\\omega t) \\\\hat{i}$. The corresponding magnetic field $\\\\vec{B}$ is oriented along:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$+\\\\hat{j}$ direction\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$-\\\\hat{j}$ direction\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$+\\\\hat{k}$ direction\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$-\\\\hat{i}$ direction\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Poynting vector $\\\\vec{S} \\\\propto \\\\vec{E} \\\\times \\\\vec{B}$ must point along propagation direction $\\\\hat{k}$. Since $\\\\vec{E}$ is along $\\\\hat{i}$, $\\\\hat{i} \\\\times \\\\hat{j} = \\\\hat{k}$, so $\\\\vec{B}$ is along $+\\\\hat{j}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
        }
