          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A Carnot engine has an efficiency of $\\\\eta = 40\\\\%$ when its sink is at $T_c = 300\\\\text{ K}$. To increase the efficiency to $50\\\\%$, keeping sink temperature constant, the source temperature must be raised by:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$100\\\\text{ K}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$50\\\\text{ K}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$200\\\\text{ K}$\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$150\\\\text{ K}$\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Initial: $1 - \\\\frac{300}{T_h} = 0.4 \\\\implies T_h = 500\\\\text{ K}$. Desired: $1 - \\\\frac{300}{T_h\\\'} = 0.5 \\\\implies T_h\\\' = 600\\\\text{ K}$. Rise $\\\\Delta T_h = 600 - 500 = 100\\\\text{ K}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 20,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section A\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'MCQ\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A plane electromagnetic wave propagating along the $+z$ direction has its electric field given by $\\\\vec{E} = E_0 \\\\cos(kz - \\\\omega t) \\\\hat{i}$. The corresponding magnetic field $\\\\vec{B}$ is oriented along:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    options: ["
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'1\', label: \'$+\\\\hat{j}$ direction\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'2\', label: \'$-\\\\hat{j}$ direction\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'3\', label: \'$+\\\\hat{k}$ direction\' },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      { id: \'4\', label: \'$-\\\\hat{i}$ direction\' }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    ],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Poynting vector $\\\\vec{S} \\\\propto \\\\vec{E} \\\\times \\\\vec{B}$ must point along propagation direction $\\\\hat{k}$. Since $\\\\vec{E}$ is along $\\\\hat{i}$, $\\\\hat{i} \\\\times \\\\hat{j} = \\\\hat{k}$, so $\\\\vec{B}$ is along $+\\\\hat{j}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: -1 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  // Section B: Physics Numerical (21 - 25)"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 21,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section B\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'NUMERICAL\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A solid sphere of mass $M = 5\\\\text{ kg}$ and radius $R = 0.2\\\\text{ m}$ rolls without slipping down an inclined plane of angle $\\\\theta = 30^\\\\circ$. The linear acceleration of the center of mass of the sphere is $a = \\\\frac{n}{7} g \\\\sin\\\\theta$. Find the integer value of $n$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'5\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'For pure rolling on incline, $a = \\\\frac{g \\\\sin\\\\theta}{1 + I_{\\\\text{cm}}/(MR^2)}$. For solid sphere, $I_{\\\\text{cm}} = \\\\frac{2}{5} MR^2$, so $a = \\\\frac{g \\\\sin\\\\theta}{1 + 2/5} = \\\\frac{5}{7} g \\\\sin\\\\theta$. Hence $n = 5$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: 0 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 22,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section B\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'NUMERICAL\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A wire of length $L = 1\\\\text{ m}$ carries a current of $I = 2\\\\text{ A}$ in a uniform magnetic field $B = 0.5\\\\text{ T}$ perpendicular to the wire. The magnetic force on the wire in Newtons is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'$F = I L B \\\\sin(90^\\\\circ) = 2 \\\\times 1 \\\\times 0.5 = 1\\\\text{ N}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: 0 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 23,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section B\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'NUMERICAL\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'In a hydrogen-like atom, the electron transitions from the $n = 3$ state to the $n = 1$ state. The ratio of de Broglie wavelength in state 3 to state 1 (i.e. $\\\\lambda_3 / \\\\lambda_1$) is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'3\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Bohr orbit condition gives $2\\\\pi r_n = n \\\\lambda_n$. Velocity $v_n \\\\propto 1/n$, and de Broglie wavelength $\\\\lambda = h / (mv) \\\\propto n$. Thus $\\\\lambda_3 / \\\\lambda_1 = 3 / 1 = 3$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: 0 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 24,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section B\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'NUMERICAL\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'Two soap bubbles of radii $r_1 = 3\\\\text{ cm}$ and $r_2 = 4\\\\text{ cm}$ combine in vacuum under isothermal conditions to form a single spherical bubble of radius $R$. The radius $R$ in cm is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'5\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'Surface energy conservation or pressure relation: $4\\\\pi r_1^2 + 4\\\\pi r_2^2 = 4\\\\pi R^2 \\\\implies R = \\\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\\\\text{ cm}$.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: 0 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: 25,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subject: \'Physics\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    section: \'Section B\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \'NUMERICAL\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    questionText: \'A vernier calliper has 10 divisions on the vernier scale coinciding with 9 divisions on the main scale. If $1\\\\text{ MSD} = 1\\\\text{ mm}$, the least count of the vernier calliper in units of $10^{-1}\\\\text{ mm}$ is:\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    correctAnswer: \'1\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    explanation: \'$\\\\text{Least Count} = 1\\\\text{ MSD} - 1\\\\text{ VSD} = 1\\\\text{ mm} - 0.9\\\\text{ mm} = 0.1\\\\text{ mm} = 1 \\\\times 10^{-1}\\\\text{ mm}$. Answer is 1.\',"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    marks: { correct: 4, incorrect: 0 }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
